The west coast is not only about lying on the sand. Within a short drive you can climb a UNESCO-listed mountain, walk through the island's last native forests, sail across a turquoise lagoon and learn how the coast's history shaped Mauritius.

## Climb Le Morne Brabant

[Le Morne Brabant](/en/places/le-morne-brabant) rises 556 metres above the lagoon at the south-west tip of the island. The hike starts with an easy walk and ends with a steep scramble, with views over the reef and the lagoon. Access to the upper part has been regulated at times, so check the current rules and consider a local guide — who will also tell you the mountain's story.

## Remember at the Slave Route Monument

At the foot of the mountain, the [Slave Route Monument](/en/places/slave-route-monument) honours the people who escaped slavery and found refuge on Le Morne. It is a moving place, and essential to understanding why the mountain matters so much to Mauritians.

## Walk in Black River Gorges

[Black River Gorges National Park](/en/places/black-river-gorges-national-park) covers about 67 km² of forest and ravines, with some 60 km of trails. It is where the island's rarest birds — the Mauritius kestrel, the pink pigeon and the echo parakeet — were saved from extinction. For a bigger challenge, the [Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire](/en/places/piton-de-la-petite-riviere-noire) is the highest summit in Mauritius, at 828 metres.

## Sail across the lagoon

From La Gaulette, boats head out to [Île aux Bénitiers](/en/places/ile-aux-benitiers) and the [Crystal Rock](/en/places/crystal-rock), a fossil coral rock rising from crystal-clear shallows. Many trips leave early to look for dolphins on the way.

## See dolphins — responsibly

Spinner and bottlenose dolphins are often seen in the bays of Tamarin and Black River in the early morning. Choose a small operator that keeps its distance, limits time with the animals and does not chase or surround them. The bays are where the dolphins rest after feeding at night.

## Explore the coast's history

The [Martello tower at La Preneuse](/en/places/martello-tower-la-preneuse), built by the British in 1832–1833, tells of the years when Britain feared a French return. Down the road, the [Tamarin salt pans](/en/places/tamarin-salt-pans) recall the way the coast once made its living from the sun and the sea.

## Ride the wind

Le Morne is one of the world's best-known kitesurfing spots, with flat water inside the lagoon for beginners and a reef wave for experts. The wind is strongest and most reliable in the southern winter. Learn with a certified school: the lagoon is large, and conditions change fast.
